{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "String Data Type in Java"}, {"type": "position", "content": 4}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use computer programming language."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, and characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discuss a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob04re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Questions with How + Adjectives"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use questions with the <i><b>how + adjective</i></b> form to ask about the degree of strength of the adjective involved in the sentence.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "To use questions with the <i><b>how+ adjective</i></b> form follow the next structure: </br></br>                [How +Adjective + Verb+ Subject+ Complement] </br>      <strong>How important  </strong>   is </b>      string in computer programming language?  </br> </br> Notice that in this type of questions we don't use an auxiliary verb such as Do/ Does. As this is a way of asking for the description of something or someone, we have to use <i><b>the verb to be.</i></b></br></br>   <li><i><strong>How efficient is</strong> this method? </br> Well, it's not that efficient. We should try another one. </br> <li><i><strong>How important are</strong>  strings in Java? </br> They are quite important since Java uses them in its codes.   "}, {"type": "example", "content": "How efficient is this method?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "How detrimental is this method?"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "String Methods "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Strings, which are widely used in many programming languages, are a sequence of characters that Java uses as objects on its codes. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "A string is a data type applied in programming which is used to represent texts.  It consists of sequences of characters or codes called escape codes. A string can be a word like \u201cgood\u201d or a phrase \u201cThis is good\u201d. The data has to be enclosed in quotation marks to be recognized as a string, and once it is created, it can't be modified. Java string data is also considered as a class which is the reason why it has methods. Let's have a look at them.</br></br>  <b>String Length () Method</b></br> It is an accessor method used to get information of an object. Using the string length method you can get the number of characters, including spaces, the string object has. </br></br>  <b>Upper and Lower Case Methods</b></br> They are used to return the original string in upper case or lower case depending on the case. </br></br>  <b>Trim</b></br> This method is helpful to get rid of the extra spaces of the string. </br></br>   <b>Replace ()</b></br> As its name implies, this method is helpful to replace an instance with another one. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "immutable "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that doesn't change or vary "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Don't forget that strings in java are immutable. This means that once you've created a string you can never change it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob04aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "reassign "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to put something again in a place"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Then, you reassign the keyword to the new string. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob04aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": " efficient "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "Someone or something competent that gets the intended results"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: How efficient is this method?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob04aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "Java "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "A computer programming language designed for Internet"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I read that a string is a sequence of characters considered as objects in Java."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob04aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "concatenation"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the act of linking together "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We use concatenation (+) at the end of each keyword to relate the sequences. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob04aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "detrimental "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that causes a damage in a process  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: How detrimental is this method?  "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob04aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for extra information about string usages in Java programming language."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write notes and create a brochure to describe the string usages and main features."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share the brochure with your instructor and answer all his questions"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for extra information about string usages in Java programming language."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a brochure to describe the string usages and main features."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk about string usages and main features in Java programming language."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob04re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<b>Teacher: </b> Let's talk about strings in Java. Does anybody know what a string is? </br><b>Student: </b> I read that a string is a sequence of characters considered as objects in Java. </br><b>Teacher</b>: Exactly! Don't forget that strings in Java are immutable. This means that once you've created a string you can never change it. Before we continue, do you remember what   syntax is? </br><b>Student: </b>When you declare a string you need to know about syntax. It is basically a set of rules -with variables and types of characters- that define and state how a Java program is written. </br>"}, {"time": "38000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob04re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<b>Teacher: </b> Good! Let's continue. It's very common to create a new string with a string literal, which is a group of characters that are enclosed in double quotes ( \u201c \u201c). Remember that = \u201c \u201c   is equal to an empty space. Then, you reassign the keyword to the new string + = \u201cMy name is Bob.\u201d Remember that we can add strings together, so we use concatenation (+) at the end of each keyword to relate the sequences.  </br><b>Student: </b> How efficient is this method? </br><b>Teacher: </b> I have to say that this method is quite inefficient because it starts with a single character and an empty space = \u201c \u201c .This will be an empty space forever. Every time we create a new string and reassign the keyword, we're creating a new string made of the previous one. </br>"}, {"time": "97000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob04re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<b>Student: </b> How detrimental is this method? </br><b>Teacher: </b>\nIf you're writing a big program, this method could slow it down or use a lot of memory, so, to get around this, we should use another method.\nWe should create a string with content. Let's say \u201cnew StringBuilder\u201d and then we reassign a keyword. In this way we're not creating a new string but we're modifying the content of the existent one. </br><b>Student: </b> What about lower or upper case in Java? How should I type? </br>\n<b>Teacher: </b> Well, to make lower or upper case in Java just type in <i>toUpperCase </i>or <i>toLowerCase</i>."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le02ob04re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le02ob04re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can say what a string in Java is. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can talk about the syntax to use in the string data type.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can say how concatenation works in the string data type. "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you consider Java is necessary when using your computer? Why?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": " As a programmer, why is it important to learn and identify the syntax of certain programming tools? "}], "type": "instructions"}